                <h2>Clear Conclusion: Tier Accounts Before Distributing Budget</h2>
<p>The main challenge for a multi-account advertising team is not simply operating more accounts. It is failing to define clear boundaries between them. Core channels and experimental campaigns may share the same budget, account owners may have excessive permissions, recharge activity may go unreconciled, and creative reviews may lack consistent standards. The result is predictable: inefficient accounts continue consuming funds while valuable campaigns run out of balance or miss launch windows.</p>
<p>Account tiering should therefore function as core operating infrastructure. At a minimum, accounts can be divided into four levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Core tier:</strong> Proven products and markets that receive priority funding and balance protection;</li>
<li><strong>Growth tier:</strong> Campaigns using validated structures for creative expansion, new audiences, and budget scaling, with clear stop-loss conditions;</li>
<li><strong>Testing tier:</strong> Isolated accounts for new platforms, countries, audiences, or landing pages, each with a controlled testing budget;</li>
<li><strong>Risk tier:</strong> Restricted, under-review, abnormal-spending, or potentially harmful accounts that require immediate investigation.</li>
</ul>
<p>These tiers should remain dynamic. An account belonging to a utility app may move from testing to growth after a new creative improves performance. Conversely, unusual spending should trigger an immediate downgrade and a dedicated review.</p>
<h2>Use One Management Matrix to Reduce Internal Friction</h2>
<p>Every account should have a management record covering the platform, region, product, account ID, owner, tier, daily budget, balance status, creative batch, and latest review result.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Core Tier</th>
<th>Growth Tier</th>
<th>Testing Tier</th>
<th>Risk Tier</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Budget approach</td>
<td>Priority allocation</td>
<td>Scale when targets are met</td>
<td>Fixed testing allowance</td>
<td>Restrict additional spend</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Permissions</td>
<td>Owner plus approval</td>
<td>Adjustable within limits</td>
<td>No unapproved increases</td>
<td>Restricted handling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Recharging</td>
<td>Automated alerts and manual checks</td>
<td>Trigger-based reminders</td>
<td>Small, staged top-ups</td>
<td>Automatic recharge paused</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Changes</td>
<td>Supervisor approval</td>
<td>Owner can adjust</td>
<td>Test rationale required</td>
<td>Dedicated personnel only</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Account names should also follow a consistent format, such as “platform–product–region–purpose–date.” Clear naming helps align creative reports, recharge records, and refund checks. It prevents teams from discovering during month-end reconciliation that billing data cannot be matched with campaign ownership.</p>
<h2>Daily SOP: Embed Controls Into the Campaign Workflow</h2>
<h3>1. Verify the Spending Plan Before Recharging</h3>
<p>Account owners should submit a recharge request indicating its purpose and the accounts that will use the funds. Finance or account operations should then verify the balance, currency, payment entity, and applicable taxes before the payment is made. Multi-account teams should avoid relying on informal payment screenshots. Instead, they need a documented sequence covering request, approval, receipt, and verification, including confirmation that the funds reached the correct advertising account.</p>
<h3>2. Replace One-Size-Fits-All Rules With Tier-Specific Thresholds</h3>
<p>Core accounts should be monitored for balance coverage, conversion cost, and review status. Testing accounts need closer attention to click costs, landing-page conversion, and short-term spending velocity. Risk accounts require review of abnormal consumption spikes, chargebacks, rejections, and platform warnings. Thresholds should reflect historical volatility rather than applying the same percentage to every account.</p>
<h3>3. Apply Two Checks Before Creative Activation</h3>
<p>The creative owner should confirm the selling point, asset version, and intended audience. The account owner should then verify usage rights, landing-page consistency, and audience settings. In larger teams, new assets for core accounts should be reviewed before scaling, while testing accounts can iterate quickly within clearly defined limits.</p>
<h2>When Something Goes Wrong, Downgrade First and Investigate Second</h2>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team once managed advertising accounts across several regions. It consolidated spending every week, but balances remained opaque. Before a seasonal peak, an owner increased limits across multiple accounts to prevent interruptions. The team later discovered unusually rapid spending in one testing account but could not determine whether it came from auction changes or tracking issues.</p>
<p>The team introduced an owner for each account, balance alerts, tier-specific spending limits, and stronger separation between testing and core accounts. When abnormal spending appeared, it could pause the testing account first and review event logs, authorized access, and creative changes without disrupting the entire operation.</p>
<p>This example shows that account tiering is not merely a cleaner reporting method. It tells the team which account to act on first when a problem occurs. Effective tiering is a dynamic operating mechanism that uses campaign objectives, performance, and risk status to move accounts between levels. It integrates recharging, permissions, creative review, and reconciliation into one workflow. The more accounts a team operates, the more it needs standardization—but those standards should not become rigid. Maintaining room for fast, controlled testing is the practical balance between efficiency and risk.</p>
